🧱 Basic Concepts

Object:
Anything stored in AD — like users, computers, printers, or folders.

Attributes:
Details about each object.
Example: A user has attributes like name, email, phone number, etc.

Schema:
Think of it like a blueprint of AD. It defines what types of objects exist and what attributes they can have.

🌐 Structure

Domain:
A group of related objects (users, computers, etc.) — like a “city.”

Forest:
A collection of one or more domains — like a “country.”

Tree:
A set of domains that share a common root — like branches of the same tree.

Container / Leaf:

Containers hold other objects (like folders).

Leaf objects are the “end” items (like a user or printer).

🧭 Identification

GUID (Global Unique ID):
A permanent, unique ID for every object in AD.

SID (Security Identifier):
A unique ID for security-related objects like users or groups. Used to control access.

DN (Distinguished Name):
The full “address” of an object in AD (e.g. bjones → IT → Employees → inlanefreight.local).

RDN (Relative Distinguished Name):
The object’s name within its folder (e.g. just bjones).

sAMAccountName:
The short username (max 20 characters) — used to log in.

userPrincipalName (UPN):
The login in email format (e.g. bjones@inlanefreight.local).

🏛️ Domain Controllers & Operations

FSMO Roles:
Special roles given to domain controllers to prevent conflicts and manage AD efficiently.

Global Catalog (GC):
A “directory of directories” — helps find objects across all domains.

RODC (Read-Only Domain Controller):
A copy of AD that can’t be changed — used in remote or less secure locations.

Replication:
The process where domain controllers share and update AD data so all stay in sync.

🔐 Security

SPN (Service Principal Name):
Identifies services in Kerberos authentication (e.g. linking a service to its account).

ACL (Access Control List):
A list of who can access what.

ACE (Access Control Entry):
An entry in the ACL that says who is allowed or denied.

DACL:
Defines who can access an object.

SACL:
Logs who tried to access an object.

🧩 Other Key Terms

FQDN (Fully Qualified Domain Name):
The full name of a computer or host (e.g. DC01.inlanefreight.local).

Tombstone:
A temporary “holding area” for deleted objects (they can be recovered before expiry).

AD Recycle Bin:
Makes restoring deleted AD objects easier and keeps their attributes.

SYSVOL:
A shared folder on domain controllers containing scripts and policies.

AdminSDHolder:
Protects admin accounts and groups from unauthorized changes.

adminCount:
Marks accounts as “protected” (admin-level accounts have this set to 1).

NTDS.DIT:
The main AD database file — it stores all users, groups, and password hashes.
